Algorithm is a step-by-step method or a set of instructions to solve a problem.

# 1. Supervised Learning Algorithms

| **Algorithm**                                      | **Common Use**                                                                 |
|----------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Linear Regression**                              | Predicting continuous values (price, demand)                                   |
| **Logistic Regression**                            | Binary classification (fraud/not fraud, spam/not spam)                         |
| **Decision Trees**                                 | Classification and regression with interpretable decision rules                |
| **Random Forest**                                  | Ensemble of decision trees for improved accuracy                               |
| **Support Vector Machines (SVM)**                  | Classification, especially when classes have clear margins                     |
| **Naive Bayes**                                    | Text classification, spam filtering                                            |
| **K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)**                      | Classification based on the nearest data points                                |
| **Gradient Boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost)**| High-accuracy prediction for structured/tabular data; widely used in industry  |
| **Neural Networks (Supervised Mode)**              | Complex pattern recognition (e.g., image classification, speech recognition)   |

# 2. Unsupervised Learning Algorithms

| **Algorithm**                         | **Common Use**                                                              |
|---------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **K-Means Clustering**                | Groups similar data points (e.g., customer segmentation)                    |
| **Hierarchical Clustering**           | Creates nested groups of data; visualized using dendrograms                 |
| **DBSCAN**                            | Density-based clustering; effective for anomaly and outlier detection        |
| **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)**| Reduces data dimensions while preserving important information              |
| **Autoencoders**                      | Data compression and anomaly detection (also used in deep learning)          |
| **Association Rule Learning (Apriori)** | Discovers relationships such as "People who bought X also bought Y"       |
| **Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM)**     | Probabilistic clustering that models data as a mixture of Gaussian distributions |

# 3. Reinforcement Learning Algorithms

| **Algorithm**                        | **Common Use**                                                              |
|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Q-Learning**                       | Basic value-based reinforcement learning; learns action–value (Q-value) pairs |
| **Deep Q-Networks (DQN)**            | Combines Q-learning with deep learning; widely used in Atari-playing AI     |
| **Policy Gradient Methods**          | Directly learns the optimal action policy                                   |
| **Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)** | A widely used modern RL algorithm; used in RLHF for models like ChatGPT     |
| **Actor-Critic Methods**             | Combines value-based and policy-based reinforcement learning approaches     |
| **Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)**   | Used in AlphaGo for game-tree exploration and planning                      |

---

# 4. Semi-supervised Learning Algorithms

| **Algorithm**                              | **Common Use**                                                          |
|--------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Self-training (Pseudo-labeling)**        | Model generates labels for unlabeled data and retrains using them       |
| **Label Propagation**                      | Spreads labels from labeled data to nearby unlabeled data points        |
| **Semi-supervised Support Vector Machine (S3VM)** | Extends SVM to learn from both labeled and unlabeled data          |
| **Generative Adversarial Networks (Semi-supervised GANs)** | GANs adapted to learn with partially labeled datasets |

# 5. Self-supervised Learning Algorithms

| **Algorithm**                     | **Common Use**                                                                 |
|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Masked Language Modeling (MLM)** | BERT-style training; predicts masked or hidden words in a sentence            |
| **Next Token/Word Prediction**     | GPT/Llama-style training; predicts the next word/token (used to train Llama 3, Claude, ChatGPT) |
| **Contrastive Learning (SimCLR, CLIP)** | Learns representations by comparing similar and dissimilar pairs; widely used in image-text models |
| **Autoregressive Pretraining**     | Core technique behind LLMs; generates text one token at a time based on previously generated tokens |

